// The `prelude.reflection` sub-module: agents as first-class, inspectable values. Called qualified
// via the default import — `reflection.get_metadata`, `reflection.call_agent`, type
// `reflection.agent_metadata`.
//
// The two primitives close one loop: `get_metadata` reads a callable's public signature (so an AI
// can be shown what a tool accepts) and `call_agent` dispatches a callable value with a
// runtime-built argument (so an AI's pick can be invoked). Callables whose public signature is
// decided at RUNTIME — an MCP server's tools (`prelude.mcp`) — are minted by the runtime itself and
// participate in both exactly like compiled agents: a tool IS an agent.
//
// `get_metadata` derives a callable value's public signature — its qualified name, its `@"..."`
// description, and the JSON Schemas of its input / output / requests, each schema a plain document
// value (a record / array tree). To assemble a tool list, build the surrounding document with ordinary
// record / array literals, embedding each schema by plain assignment — e.g.
// `{ name = m.name, input_schema = m.input }`. Each schema is already a document value, so it drops
// straight into the surrounding record; there is no wire step to apply.
//
// `call_agent` dispatches a callable VALUE with a runtime-built argument record — the
// dynamic-dispatch counterpart of a static call, for a target an AI picked out of a tool list. The
// runtime validates every delegation's argument against the callee's input schema, so malformed args
// throw `call_error` at the call site rather than corrupting the callee — an AI-driven tool loop can
// catch it and let the model retry with corrected arguments.
//
// `describe` writes to the same description channel `get_metadata` reads, at RUNTIME: it is the
// value-level twin of a documented let (`@"..." let tool = some_agent`), which already stamps the
// bound value's naming. Without it, the description an AI is shown must be a source literal, so a
// protocol paragraph shared by a dozen tools is a dozen copies that drift apart; with it, the shared
// paragraph is one ordinary string value composed into each tool's text by f-string.

@"The public metadata of one callable: its (qualified) name, its `@\"...\"` description, and the JSON
Schemas of its input, output and requests — each schema a plain document value, ready to embed in an AI
tool list. A local (closure) callable has an empty name."
data agent_metadata(
  @"The qualified name; empty for a local closure." name: string,
  @"The `@\"...\"` documentation text; empty when undocumented." description: string,
  @"The input record's JSON Schema, as a document value." input: unknown,
  @"The output's JSON Schema, as a document value." output: unknown,
  @"The requests' schemas: one { name, input, output } object per request the callable may perform." requests: unknown,
)

@"Derive `agent_metadata` from a callable value (a top-level agent, a data constructor, a request, an
external, a local closure, or a runtime-minted tool such as an MCP server's). A generic callable
instantiated with `[T]` yields schemas specialised to the instantiation; a runtime-minted tool yields
the name / description / input schema its provider declared."
primitive agent get_metadata(@"The callable value to inspect." value: agent never -> unknown with all) -> agent_metadata

@"Re-describe a callable VALUE: hand back the same callable carrying @description@ as the text
`get_metadata` — and every tool listing built from it — reports. This is the runtime twin of a
documented let (`@\"...\" let tool = some_agent`), and it obeys the same value-level rule: a
re-description RENAMES nothing and RE-TYPES nothing. The input / output / request schemas stay the
target's own, the qualified name stays what it was, and calling the result runs exactly the same
agent. Only the sentence the model reads changes.

Reach for it when one paragraph — a protocol, a safety rule, a shared vocabulary — belongs in the
description of many tools. Written as source `@\"...\"` text that paragraph must be copied per tool
and drifts; here it is an ordinary string value, composed into each tool's description with an
f-string (`describe(target = close_ticket, description = f\"${own_text} ${protocol}\")`) so the shared
half has exactly one home. Pure: the answer is a COPY, so the original binding keeps its old
description — bind the result and hand that around."
primitive agent describe[F extends agent never -> unknown with all](
  @"The callable value to re-describe." target: F,
  @"The description text the model should read for this tool." description: string,
) -> F

@"Reify T's JSON Schema as a plain document value — the same schema `json.validate[T]` checks a value
against and `get_metadata` embeds for a callable's input / output. The reified schema is what a type
looks like FROM OUTSIDE (an AI told what shape to produce, a tool list assembled by hand): an object
type lists its fields under `required`, a data value nests under `$katari_constructor` /
`$katari_value`, a `file` is its `$katari_ref` handle object. Instantiate T explicitly — it appears
nowhere in the signature, so it can never be inferred."
primitive agent schema_of[T]() -> unknown

// ── constructor_of: a VALUE's own tag, for a program that only has `unknown` ──────────────────────
//
// `get_metadata` answers what a CALLABLE is; this answers what a VALUE is. The two are different
// questions and only the first had an agent: a supervisor holding a caught throw, a converter reading a
// crash payload, a tool loop rendering an AI's reply all hold an `unknown` whose SHAPE they cannot
// enumerate — the whole point of a supervisor is that it survives errors nobody declared — and the one
// stable fact such a value carries is the name of the `data` constructor that built it.
//
// A `match` cannot ask that question: a `case my_error(...)` arm requires the type to be NAMED in source,
// which is precisely what the caller does not have. So the name was read as a magic string instead, and
// every app that needed it grew its own copy of the wire key. This is that key's one home.

@"The name of the `data` constructor that built @value@ — the fully qualified name (`prelude.http.auth_error`),
or `null` when @value@ is not a `data` value at all (a bare string, a number, a plain record, an array).

Reach for it where the SHAPE is genuinely unknown and only the IDENTITY is needed: a supervisor deciding
whether this crash is a repeat of the last one, a converter routing an uncaught payload, a log line that
should say WHAT failed without pretending to know its fields. Comparing rendered messages instead is the
trap this replaces — a message quoting an id or an instant never equals the previous one, so a standing
outage reads as a fresh failure every time.

It is an IDENTITY, not a render: the answer is stable across two values of the same constructor however
differently their fields read, and it is not a sentence to show anyone. To SEE a value, use
`json.stringify`; to READ its fields, match on it or `json.validate` it into a type you name."
agent constructor_of(@"The value to identify; any value, and a non-`data` one answers `null`." value: unknown) -> string | null {
  // The constructor tag is OUT OF BAND on a value — it is not a field, so no `record.get` and no `match`
  // can reach it. Its one observable appearance is the wire form `json.stringify` renders (a data value
  // becomes `{"$katari_constructor": name, "$katari_value": {...}}`, the tag first), so the tag is read
  // back out of that text here — ONCE, in the stdlib, instead of in every app that needs an identity.
  // Total by construction: anything that is not that exact shape answers `null`, and a constructor name
  // is a qualified identifier, so the name never contains the quote this scans for.
  let tag_prefix = "{\"$katari_constructor\":\""
  let rendered = json.stringify(value = value)
  if (!string.starts_with(value = rendered, search = tag_prefix)) {
    null
  } else {
    let tail = string.slice(value = rendered, start = string.length(value = tag_prefix), end = string.length(value = rendered))
    match (string.index_of(value = tail, search = "\"")) {
      case null -> null
      case end -> string.slice(value = tail, start = 0, end = end)
    }
  }
}

@"A dynamic dispatch failed before the target ran: the target is not a callable, or `args` does not
conform to its input schema (`message` names the offending path). Thrown by `call_agent`."
data call_error(@"The non-callable kind, or the offending path of the schema mismatch." message: string)

@"Invoke a callable VALUE with a runtime-built argument record — dynamic dispatch for a target an AI
picked out of a tool list. `args` is validated against the target's input schema at the delegation
boundary; a mismatch throws `call_error` at this call site (catch it to let the model retry). The
target's effects flow through unchanged.

FILE HANDLES PASS STRAIGHT THROUGH. `args` is an ordinary value tree (records / arrays / scalars), and a
`$katari_ref` handle object anywhere inside it is ALREADY a real `file` value — the wire-to-value
conversion happens once, unconditionally, wherever a JSON document crosses into the program (a model
reply read by `json.parse`, an `mcp` result, an FFI return), and is never schema-directed. So a model
that replays a handle it saw earlier in the conversation satisfies a `file` parameter with nothing to
copy wrong, and the schema check here sees the `file` the target asked for rather than a bare record."
primitive agent call_agent[R, effect E](
  @"The callable value to dispatch (e.g. the tool an AI picked)." target: agent never -> R with E,
  @"The target's whole argument record, keyed by its parameter names." args: unknown,
) -> R with E | prelude.throw[call_error]
